    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-54ea922066b6\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"no-fork-bomb-command\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\":\\\\(\\\\)\\\\s*\\\\{\\\\s*:\\\\|:&\\\\s*\\\\}\\\\s*;:\",\"path\":\".floor/command.txt\"}],\"coverage\":\"Decides every tool call with a string shell command in which the fork-bomb text `:(){ :|:& };:` (with optional whitespace) sits on one line of the command. This is the rule's regex applied verbatim to the command.\",\"residual\":\"Does the one-line description of this call match the fork-bomb regex in a way the per-line command check misses? That is: does the command spread the match across a newline, or, for a call without a shell command, do the tool name plus file path, or the tool name plus input, contain the pattern?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":{\"tool_name\":\"Bash\",\"tool_input\":{\"command\":\":(){ :|:& };:\"}},\"compliant\":{\"tool_name\":\"Bash\",\"tool_input\":{\"command\":\"ls -la\"}}},\"rationale\":\"The rule's regex runs verbatim on command.txt, which is exact for single-line shell commands. Calls without a command are described by tool name plus file path, or tool name plus input, in a rendering the contract does not specify. Scanning tool-call.json would also wrongly fire on content fields. A command containing newlines could match through \\\\s in the description but not line by line. So these cases are left as residual.\"}",
